{"name":"Ostrich Tail","hex":"#eadfe6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ostrich","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large flightless bird of the order Struthioniformes.\nThe most widespread species of that order, known as the common ostrich (Struthio camelus).","The most widespread species of that order, known as the common ostrich (Struthio camelus)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ostrich"},{"term":"tail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The caudal appendage of an animal that is attached to their posterior and near the anus or cloaca.","An object or part of an object resembling a tail in shape, such as the thongs on a cat-o'-nine-tails; a strand of material hanging from something."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hold by the end; said of a timber when it rests upon a wall or other support; with in or into","To swing with the stern in a certain direction; said of a vessel at anchor."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ostrich","blocks":[{"text":"Ostriches are large flightless birds. Two living species are recognised: the common ostrich, native to large parts of Sub-Saharan Africa, and the Somali ostrich, native to the Horn of Africa."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Struthio_Diversity.jpg/330px-Struthio_Diversity.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ostrich","wikidata":"Q2576337"},{"title":"Tail","blocks":[{"text":"The tail is the elongated section at the rear end of a bilaterian animal's body; in general, the term refers to a distinct, flexible appendage extending backwards from the midline of the torso. In vertebrate animals that evolved to lose their tails, the coccyx is the homologous vestigial of the tail. While tails are primarily considered a feature of vertebrates, some invertebrates such as scorpions and springtails, as well as snails and slugs, have tail-like appendages that are also referred to as tails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/White-tailed_deer%2C_tail_up.jpg/330px-White-tailed_deer%2C_tail_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tail","wikidata":"Q60960"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Struthio_camelus_MHNT.ZOO.2010.11.1.1.jpg/250px-Struthio_camelus_MHNT.ZOO.2010.11.1.1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Struthio_camelus_MHNT.ZOO.2010.11.1.1.jpg/500px-Struthio_camelus_MHNT.ZOO.2010.11.1.1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Struthio_camelus_MHNT.ZOO.2010.11.1.1.jpg/250px-Struthio_camelus_MHNT.ZOO.2010.11.1.1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStruthio_camelus_MHNT.ZOO.2010.11.1.1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Ostrich_%26_chicken_egg_comparison.jpg/250px-Ostrich_%26_chicken_egg_comparison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Ostrich_%26_chicken_egg_comparison.jpg/500px-Ostrich_%26_chicken_egg_comparison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Ostrich_%26_chicken_egg_comparison.jpg/250px-Ostrich_%26_chicken_egg_comparison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOstrich_%26_chicken_egg_comparison.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Ostrich_with_eggs.jpg/250px-Ostrich_with_e